00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.310
about more in a little bit.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.000
I also work on our
systems that handle

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.130
alerting and
notifications, kind of how

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:37.910
you are able to tell the system,
hey, alert me and notify me

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:39.770
if something's going
wrong with my system.

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.460
That's kind of my focus area.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.210
JON FOUST: Gotcha.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.627
So that sounds
really interesting,

00:06:43.627 --> 00:06:46.250
because I just started using
Stackdriver myself recently,

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.380
because like many
of our developers,

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.380
they just want to build
the solution right away.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.583
But when things go wacky or
when things are uploaded,

00:06:53.583 --> 00:06:55.250
they don't really
actually have the time

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:57.890
to follow up and
actually monitor

00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.480
and actually get these alerts.

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:02.240
So I recently just
started using alerts

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.412
for when my Noogler project goes
off, whenever anything fails.

00:07:05.412 --> 00:07:07.370
So can you tell our
listeners a little bit more

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:09.812
about the type of alerts
that you actually get.

00:07:09.812 --> 00:07:10.520
RORY PETTY: Sure.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.153
So there's kind of
a variety of alerts

00:07:13.153 --> 00:07:15.320
that you can set up with
the Stackdriver Monitoring.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.960
So I'll start out by
saying that, in terms

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:20.710
of all the Google Cloud
Services that are out there,

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.460
Stackdriver supports them--

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:24.380
Stackdriver
Monitoring and Logging

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:25.640
supports them out of the box.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.850
So for example, if you spin
up a new compute instance,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.870
a new compute VM, or you spin
up an App Engine instance,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:35.120
all that kind of stuff,
they automatically start

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.180
sending metrics and logs
to Stackdriver for you.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:41.030
So you really, in
most cases, for a VM,

00:07:41.030 --> 00:07:43.890
you might have to install an
agent or a couple other things.

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:46.340
But you're basically getting
out-of-the-box metrics

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:47.300
and logs.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.950
And so I like to think
about it in a couple stages.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:54.020
First is I'm receiving this kind
of telemetry data from my VM,

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.280
from my application.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:57.620
Just let me look at the
data and understand it.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:03.320
So that could be I'm looking at
CPU usage or network latency.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:05.030
It could be something
more complicated.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.420
If I've created
SLOs for my service.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.273
But basically you're
getting this data,

00:08:10.273 --> 00:08:11.690
you're kind of
figuring out what's

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.170
the steady state of
your system, and then

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.760
what's the state of
your system where

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.260
it's going to start to degrade?

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:23.600
So for example, if your
CPU is at 100% on the VM

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.330
and you can't accept
new incoming requests,

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.607
or if you run out
of storage, or any

00:08:28.607 --> 00:08:30.690
of the million different
things that could happen.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.479
So that's where
alerts come into play.

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:34.909
It's where you say, OK, I
know what the correct state

00:08:34.909 --> 00:08:36.440
of my system should be.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:39.169
And if certain
metrics start to go

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:41.542
past these certain
thresholds, then

00:08:41.542 --> 00:08:44.000
alert me, because I should come
back and take a look at it,

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.375
and I should spin
up new servers,

00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:48.500
or I should figure out
if a bug was deployed,

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.680
whatever the case may be.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.470
So with alerts, there's a
couple of different approaches.

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.100
The first is you can
alert on metrics.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.800
So these would be
GCP service metrics

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.660
that the services send-- again,
App Engine, GCE, GKE, whatever.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.280
It can also be custom metrics.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:07.160
So for example, say, I
have an e-commerce store,

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:09.350
every time someone
checks out, I may

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.740
want to write a metric
to Stackdriver to say

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:12.900
a checkout just happened.

00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:17.210
So from that, later, I can
visualize these metrics.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.760
Like I could have a dashboard
of the number of checkouts

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.230
per day or per hour.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:24.110
I could do other kind
of analysis or math

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.110
on that to tell me
what's the amount of time

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:30.440
that it takes between
checkouts, or how frequently are

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.500
my high-value customers checking
out, all that kind of stuff.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.180
Another approach
that you can take,

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.190
if you've read the
Google SRE book,

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:39.710
it draws this distinction
between white-box monitoring

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:41.180
and black-box monitoring.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:45.650
So white-box monitoring is
when your service or system

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.910
omits metrics.

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.400
Like your VM is
constantly sending CPU

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:51.930
usage metrics, network
metrics, whatever.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.510
But a black box is
literally just that.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.640
You basically say, I don't know
the contents of this system,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.580
but I'm going to ping it
and say, hey, are you alive,

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:02.490
are you working?

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.410
So basically, in
Stackdriver, you

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.810
can go in and create what's
called an uptime check.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:12.270
And Google's HTTP prober service
that we run globally, it's

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:13.980
also the same
service that Google

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.590
uses to black-box monitor
its own internal tools

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:17.400
and services.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.070
It can basically
send an HTTP request

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.650
to a URL endpoint for your
app, and not only say,

00:10:23.650 --> 00:10:25.620
hey, are you up, but
it can actually check

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.040
the contents of the response.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.630
So I'm going to hit
the shopping cart URL

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.070
endpoint of my
e-commerce service,

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.590
and I'm going to
check the response

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:39.270
to say, OK, am I getting
certain HTML form fields back,

00:10:39.270 --> 00:10:40.510
am I getting certain values?

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:41.510
So you can kind of do--

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:44.340
it's basically like a next-order
set of checks to make sure

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:45.780
that your service is healthy.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:48.030
And so again, going
back to the SRE book,

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:49.920
it kind of has
this recommendation

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.950
that, for an advanced
monitoring practice,

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.090
you should really use both.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:57.000
You should have alerts set up
on the white-box metrics that

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.920
are being exported
by your service,

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.830
and then you should also have
these black-box programmers

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.400
that are probing the health
and liveliness of your service

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:05.010
as well.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:05.890
JON FOUST: Sounds awesome.

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.190
One thing that you said that
I think our listeners will

00:11:08.190 --> 00:11:09.990
be interested in
