00:12:04.605 --> 00:12:07.590
an e-commerce provider,
like Target.com

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.600
or something like that,
but it's probably,

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:12.640
at a certain scale, divided
across multiple projects.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.930
So you might have a front
end for the website that's

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:18.150
being driven by services
out of one project,

00:12:18.150 --> 00:12:22.950
you might have a shopping cart
or payment gateway service

00:12:22.950 --> 00:12:24.490
that runs in another project.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.580
So long story short,
you want to monitor

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.660
all of these kind of
in the same place.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:30.990
And so a workspace, you
can put multiple projects

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:33.840
into a workspace that can
give you visibility across all

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:34.990
of them at the same time.

00:12:34.990 --> 00:12:37.420
So Jon, going back
to your question,

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:40.145
once you go into the
Stackdriver Monitoring section,

00:12:40.145 --> 00:12:41.770
there's a variety of
things you can do.

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:45.300
One is that there's this
section called Resources,

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:48.390
where we actually create
out-of-the-box dashboards

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:52.090
for you, for a variety
of common resources.

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:56.340
So that could be Cloud Storage,
VM instances, App Engine,

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.300
Google Kubernetes
Engine, all of those.

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:01.260
So you actually don't have
to do a lot of configuration

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:02.380
out of the box.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:07.220
For example, say, let's select
one of our GCE VM instances.

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:10.020
We're going to give
you CPU usage, memory

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:11.810
usage, uptime check latency.

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.248
We're going to show you events
related to that particular VM,

00:13:15.248 --> 00:13:17.790
whether you have uptime checks
configured, what's the network

00:13:17.790 --> 00:13:18.600
traffic look like?

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:21.180
So a lot of people
are worried about, oh,

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.240
is it complicated to set up
and start using Stackdriver?

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:27.240
Again, for a variety of use
cases, it's pretty simple,

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:29.230
and kind of just
works out of the box.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.065
Now, if you have more
complicated needs,

00:13:32.065 --> 00:13:33.690
more advanced needs,
you want to set up

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.300
your own dashboards, that
is certainly a functionality

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:37.480
that we support.

00:13:37.480 --> 00:13:40.895
So you can go in, create a
custom dashboard, and say,

00:13:40.895 --> 00:13:42.270
I want to create
a dashboard that

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:45.630
tracks the health of my
shopping cart service

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:46.890
for my e-commerce website.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:50.010
So you can do a variety
of charts and graphs that,

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:53.250
again, measure any metric
that that service exports.

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:57.390
So that, again, could be CPU
disk, memory, network, custom

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:58.770
metrics that you're writing.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.890
It's really configurable
however you want.

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:03.060
Stackdriver also just
recently launched

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:05.910
a new product called service
monitoring that basically

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:08.730
went into alpha at Google
Cloud Next in San Francisco

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:09.760
back in April.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.230
So the idea behind that is that,
let's say you're running a GKE

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.770
cluster, there is an open-source
project called Istio which is

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:20.130
basically software
that helps you define--

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:23.280
basically, a mesh for
your microservices.

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.950
So it's everything
from the control plane

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.170
to metrics to how the
services talk to each other,

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:29.230
authenticate.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:31.090
Istio handles all of that.

00:14:31.090 --> 00:14:33.090
And so the cool
thing is that Istio

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:35.340
borrows a lot of
architecture from how

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:38.850
Google manages its services
and microservices internally.

00:14:38.850 --> 00:14:42.200
And so Istio, once you
configure it out of the box,

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.900
will give you metrics
and traces and a variety

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:48.240
of other telemetry that you
can use to better understand

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.200
your system.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.180
And so Service
Monitoring not only

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.280
makes it easy to
configure all of that.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.248
You can actually see it visually
in this kind of typology graph.

00:14:56.248 --> 00:14:57.915
So you can imagine,
like in Google Maps,

00:14:57.915 --> 00:15:01.640
a visual map of these are where
different points of interest

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:03.070
or locations are on the map.

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:05.090
Stackdriver Service
Monitoring will actually

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:08.240
show you a visual map of
your entire microservice

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:08.890
architecture.

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:10.980
It'll show you where
all the VMs are,

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.820
who's talking to what, how
much traffic, and like how

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:16.970
many queries per second are
going between various instances

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:17.660
and endpoints.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:18.750
It's pretty cool.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:22.260
We're hoping to get to a public
beta of it later in the year.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:24.402
We don't have a
definitive timeline yet

00:15:24.402 --> 00:15:26.360
but I think it's something
that a lot of people

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:27.920
will be very excited
to work with.

