00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:33.140
environment for you, where
you can kind of spin up

00:20:33.140 --> 00:20:35.030
any kind of test
VMs that you want,

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:37.430
spin up any Kubernetes clusters.

00:20:37.430 --> 00:20:39.890
You could create, effectively,
like a whole mirror

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:42.255
of your staging or
production systems there

00:20:42.255 --> 00:20:44.630
but in a safe environment
that, once you're done with it,

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:45.980
you can tear down.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:48.553
And you don't have to worry
about you accidentally

00:20:48.553 --> 00:20:50.720
left something running, and
you're incurring billing

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:51.440
and all of that.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:53.870
So it's a very
cost-effective and safe way

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:56.900
to better understand
the whole suite of tools

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:58.100
that Stackdriver provides.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:20:59.330
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:20:59.330 --> 00:21:01.547
And you mentioned that
this is relatively new.

00:21:01.547 --> 00:21:04.130
And you mentioned a couple times
that a couple of the products

00:21:04.130 --> 00:21:05.380
are going into alpha and beta.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:06.755
Can you tell us
a little bit more

00:21:06.755 --> 00:21:09.240
about what the roadmap for
Stackdriver's looking like?

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.198
RORY PETTY: Sure, yeah.

00:21:10.198 --> 00:21:13.220
So one thing I would say
is I would go check out

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:17.060
a lot of the announcements that
we just made in April at Google

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:18.980
Cloud Next in San Francisco.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:22.800
All of the Stackdriver talks
are available on YouTube.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:24.890
I think one of the
conference websites

00:21:24.890 --> 00:21:27.380
is like cloudonair.google.com.

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:31.400
Or basically, if you Google for
like GCP Next and Cloud OnAir,

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.650
there's a huge list
of the various talks

00:21:33.650 --> 00:21:35.902
that cover Stackdriver and
DevOps and all of that.

00:21:35.902 --> 00:21:37.610
But a couple of things
that we announced.

00:21:37.610 --> 00:21:40.830
One is that our
profiler tool went GA.

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:43.460
So we haven't talked
about Profiler a lot yet,

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.710
but it's also a really
cool tool to understand

00:21:45.710 --> 00:21:47.220
the performance of your system.

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:50.570
So basically, what it does
is you basically include

00:21:50.570 --> 00:21:54.170
a library in your software and
do some basic configuration.

00:21:54.170 --> 00:21:56.490
And what it will do
is as your service

00:21:56.490 --> 00:22:00.890
is running, it will periodically
pull your CPU usage,

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:02.700
pull your memory usage.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.570
And then, basically, it
profiles your call stack

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:07.400
of your running
application, and it

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:10.410
will tell you, percentage-wise,
where you spend the most time.

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:12.560
So for example,
you could imagine

00:22:12.560 --> 00:22:16.910
you have a web server that
is receiving a request

00:22:16.910 --> 00:22:19.280
and adding an item
to a shopping cart.

00:22:19.280 --> 00:22:22.260
And so in the Profiler, you
might go in and say, OK,

00:22:22.260 --> 00:22:25.040
why is this one URL
endpoint, this API

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:26.978
handler for the shopping cart--

00:22:26.978 --> 00:22:28.520
why is it taking so
long because it's

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.470
taking like five
seconds, on average,

00:22:30.470 --> 00:22:32.540
to add an item to
the shopping cart.

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:34.640
So you go in in Profiler,
and you look at it.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:37.730
And you can clearly see, oh,
this one function or this one

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:38.330
method--

00:22:38.330 --> 00:22:42.110
say, for example like a
JSON deserialization method

00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:43.280
or something like that--

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.190
this is actually taking
up 90% of the time

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:47.820
of my total response time.

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:49.830
So as a developer,
you're able to say,

00:22:49.830 --> 00:22:52.670
oh, I need to go in and
optimize this function,

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:53.990
I need to improve my code--

00:22:53.990 --> 00:22:55.040
a variety of things.

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:58.700
So it's basically a tool
for developers that really

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:00.120
lets them optimize their code.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:01.970
And what we've
seen in practice is

00:23:01.970 --> 00:23:04.580
when big companies use
it, not only are they

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.830
able to improve the
performance of their code,

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:09.050
but that translates
back into not just

00:23:09.050 --> 00:23:11.270
benefits for their users,
but, actually, benefits

00:23:11.270 --> 00:23:12.410
for their cloud spend.

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:16.460
So again, the less time
you're spinning CPU cycles,

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:19.820
you might be able to save
money on your compute cost

00:23:19.820 --> 00:23:22.430
or the amount of memory that
you need for a particular VM

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:23.198
instance.

00:23:23.198 --> 00:23:24.740
So it can be one of
those cases where

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:27.890
a little bit of time investment
there can actually save you

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:30.740
a large amount of money or
a user satisfaction in terms

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:32.030
of real-world benefits.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:32.570
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:23:32.570 --> 00:23:34.987
I think that for the Cloud
OnAir that you were mentioning,

00:23:34.987 --> 00:23:37.740
if people can go to the Google
Cloud Platform YouTube channel,

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:39.740
if you search Stackdriver
there, you'll actually

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:41.352
see a fair amount of content.

00:23:41.352 --> 00:23:43.310
We've got the series
called Stack Doctor, where

00:23:43.310 --> 00:23:45.768
we talk about some of the best
practices in getting started

00:23:45.768 --> 00:23:48.860
with Stackdriver as well
as a bunch of webinars

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:51.592
with Cloud OnAir, where we'll
talk about different areas.

00:23:51.592 --> 00:23:53.300
I think was a recent
one on the Profiler,

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:55.700
for instance, and kind
of how those things work

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:57.200
and kind of stepping into it.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:59.120
I think there's a lot
of resources out there

00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:02.210
for, hopefully, people to
learn more about Stackdriver

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:05.040
because a lot of cases, it's not
just going to be plug and play.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.040
You're going to have to
do a fair amount of work

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:07.665
integrating it.

00:24:07.665 --> 00:24:09.915
But the more you integrate
it and kind of the more you

00:24:09.915 --> 00:24:11.363
lean into it, like
you just said,

00:24:11.363 --> 00:24:12.530
the more rewards you'll see.

00:24:12.530 --> 00:24:13.220
RORY PETTY: Yeah.

00:24:13.220 --> 00:24:15.095
And I think part of the
value of Stackdriver,

00:24:15.095 --> 00:24:18.320
too, is that it provides
a lot of these tools

00:24:18.320 --> 00:24:21.900
and services across the whole
spectrum of DevOps needs.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:23.780
So for example,
one thing that we

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:27.530
see a lot of times talking to
customers that are new to GCP--

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:30.470
they might have one tool
or service provider that

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:33.470
just does logging or another
tool that does monitoring,

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:37.740
another tool that does Trace or
Profiler-- that kind of thing.

00:24:37.740 --> 00:24:40.220
And obviously, we want
customers to use the right tools

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:41.670
based on what their needs are.

00:24:41.670 --> 00:24:44.750
But there is a value in using
a single suite of tools that

00:24:44.750 --> 00:24:46.250
are all designed
to work together

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:49.460
and can kind of get you closer
to that single pane of glass

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:52.910
that is so often talked about in
the DevOps community of what's

00:24:52.910 --> 00:24:54.950
a single place that
I can go, and I

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:57.500
can see all the telemetry.

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.150
And I can basically have a
God's eye view of my system.

00:25:00.150 --> 00:25:03.958
I can see my logs, my metrics,
my traces, all the telemetry

00:25:03.958 --> 00:25:04.500
all together.

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:05.875
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
With that being

00:25:05.875 --> 00:25:08.990
said, what would you say your
favorite piece of Stackdriver

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:09.568
is?

00:25:09.568 --> 00:25:10.610
RORY PETTY: That's tough.

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:12.290
I mean, I'm a little
biased because I

00:25:12.290 --> 00:25:14.270
work on the monitoring tool.

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:17.990
So I think that there's a lot of
value that monitoring provides.

00:25:17.990 --> 00:25:21.680
I also think that there's a
lot of value in our APM tools.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:23.570
So Trace is
particularly interesting

00:25:23.570 --> 00:25:26.120
because, again, if you have
kind of a service-based

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.680
architecture, you can
imagine a scenario-- again,

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:30.770
we'll use the shopping
cart scenario-- where

00:25:30.770 --> 00:25:32.960
customer clicks Add to Cart.

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:35.480
But behind the scenes,
there might actually

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:38.440
be tens of different services
that talk to each other.

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:41.780
So the web server has to call
the currency conversion service

00:25:41.780 --> 00:25:44.150
to convert from
dollars to euros,

00:25:44.150 --> 00:25:47.180
and then it has to call the
warehouse inventory system,

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:48.980
like is this item
still in stock.

00:25:48.980 --> 00:25:52.190
You could imagine just
one simple web request

00:25:52.190 --> 00:25:55.250
having to cascade into all
these different internal service

00:25:55.250 --> 00:25:55.820
calls.

00:25:55.820 --> 00:25:59.420
And so that's where using
traditional tools like logging

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:02.120
and debugging-- it can be
really difficult to figure out

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:04.760
what's gone wrong or where
the performance problem is

00:26:04.760 --> 00:26:08.180
because you might be looking
at hundreds of log entries

00:26:08.180 --> 00:26:09.990
across tens of
different services.

00:26:09.990 --> 00:26:13.040
So what Trace does is it gives
you this kind of waterfall view

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.350
that shows you all of
the internal service

00:26:15.350 --> 00:26:18.590
requests stitched together, so
you can very quickly and easily

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:21.710
visually see what's taking
the most time in all

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.980
of these service requests, which
internal request has the most

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:28.020
latency and is affecting the
performance of my system.

00:26:28.020 --> 00:26:31.740
So I think Trace is a really
cool tool in that regard.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:33.740
Also, a tool that
doesn't get as much

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:36.410
love, but I think is super
powerful, is Debugger.

00:26:36.410 --> 00:26:40.220
And so many people are familiar
with debuggers in the context

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:42.230
of developing your application.

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:43.580
So you're writing code.

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:44.790
You're testing it.

00:26:44.790 --> 00:26:47.750
It's not quite working right,
so you put a breakpoint

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:51.920
in your code, and then you click
a button in your website, app,

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:52.730
whatever.

00:26:52.730 --> 00:26:54.800
And then, basically,
when it reaches

00:26:54.800 --> 00:26:57.890
that point in your code,
execution will pause.

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.040
And you can see, basically,
all of the context

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:03.810
of what's going on in your
call stack at that point.

00:27:03.810 --> 00:27:06.810
So you can see what are the
value of different variables.

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:08.760
You can see a whole
variety of things.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:12.350
So again, this is a very common
workflow for a developer.

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:14.090
What's hard is that
when you're running

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:17.360
an application in production,
you can't effectively

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:18.680
pause the code.

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:20.570
If a customer is
having an issue,

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:22.280
I can't pause the
code, which pauses

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:24.800
execution for that customer
or all the customers.

00:27:24.800 --> 00:27:26.540
But what's kind of
cool and magical

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:29.840
is that if you configure
your website or application

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:31.910
to work with
Stackdriver Debugger,

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:34.820
it will effectively
take snapshots

00:27:34.820 --> 00:27:37.610
of the internal state
of your application

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:40.620
such that you can
actually link your code.

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:44.930
So if your code is in GitHub
or Google source repositories

00:27:44.930 --> 00:27:47.900
or whatever, you can actually
set breakpoints in your code,

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:50.390
and you can view
at that breakpoint

00:27:50.390 --> 00:27:53.600
the context of live
production traffic with a zero

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.690
performance hit to your actual
running web application.

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:58.280
So it's pretty magical.

00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:00.620
It basically lets you
debug production code

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.660
on the fly with no
performance hit.

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:05.360
Also, another capability
that it has is you

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:10.680
can basically use it to insert
log lines into production code.

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.850
So I mean, as developers, how
many times has this happened,

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:16.590
where you have an application,
you roll out some new code,

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:19.410
and there's a bug, or it's
not performing like it should?

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:22.530
So you say, OK, how can I
figure out what's going wrong?

00:28:22.530 --> 00:28:23.930
So you go back to your code.

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:25.550
You add a bunch more log lines.

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:27.020
You add a bunch more logging.

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:30.140
Now you have to redeploy
your entire application just

00:28:30.140 --> 00:28:33.140
to see in your logs the state
of things, what's happening,

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:34.350
what's gone wrong.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:36.710
And so where that
becomes really tricky

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:39.320
is that you've now
made it 10 times harder

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:42.380
to isolate the actual issue
because when you redeploy

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:45.638
your code, there's a huge
other variety of variables that

00:28:45.638 --> 00:28:48.180
could change, things that could
go wrong, that kind of thing.

00:28:48.180 --> 00:28:51.140
So basically, without
doing a new code deploy,

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:53.540
Debugger allows you
to insert log lines

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.870
into live running code to
better understand and diagnose

00:28:56.870 --> 00:28:57.870
and debug a problem.

00:28:57.870 --> 00:29:00.230
So I hope I did it justice
through that explanation.

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:01.520
It's a really cool tool.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:03.530
And frankly, there are
not very many other tools

00:29:03.530 --> 00:29:06.740
like that on the market
that can do live production

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:08.782
debugging with basically
zero performance impact.

00:29:08.782 --> 00:29:09.740
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:11.940
I mean, I think that's a
dream for a lot of people.

00:29:11.940 --> 00:29:13.460
But if I understood
you correctly,

00:29:13.460 --> 00:29:15.140
your favorite part
about Stackdriver

00:29:15.140 --> 00:29:17.810
is basically all of Stackdriver.

00:29:17.810 --> 00:29:18.980
RORY PETTY: Yeah.

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:21.600
I feel like I kind of
dodged your question

00:29:21.600 --> 00:29:22.813
and talked about everything.

00:29:22.813 --> 00:29:24.230
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, it's cool.

00:29:24.230 --> 00:29:25.730
There's a lot of good
stuff in there, right?

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:26.397
RORY PETTY: Yes.

00:29:26.397 --> 00:29:27.323
Yes.

00:29:27.323 --> 00:29:28.490
It's hard to pick favorites.

00:29:28.490 --> 00:29:31.160
And again, I think
it goes back to what

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:33.673
I think is a common
misunderstanding, that you

00:29:33.673 --> 00:29:35.090
should really think
of Stackdriver

00:29:35.090 --> 00:29:36.170
as separate products.

00:29:36.170 --> 00:29:39.020
I mean, there is, again, logging
and monitoring and all of that.

00:29:39.020 --> 00:29:41.300
But they really are
meant to work together

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:42.300
as a suite of tools.

00:29:42.300 --> 00:29:47.210
So don't think of logging as a
hammer and monitoring as a saw.

00:29:47.210 --> 00:29:50.960
Think of it as these are all
the tools in a toolbox that I'm

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:54.110
using to repair my house,
build a house, whatever

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:56.030
your hacky analogy is there.

00:29:56.030 --> 00:29:58.650
But they really are a suite of
tools meant to work together.

00:29:58.650 --> 00:30:00.570
JON FOUST: Your explanation
was spot-on for me

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:03.840
because I've actually used it in
the same exact scenario, where

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:07.000
I needed to test something, and
I was working with a teammate.

00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:10.200
So we were able
to not only throw

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:12.420
in log lines in the
middle of our code

00:30:12.420 --> 00:30:15.210
without having to
actually change anything.

00:30:15.210 --> 00:30:18.090
And using the Debugger, we
found several edge cases

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:19.230
that we definitely missed.

00:30:19.230 --> 00:30:21.300
And we have an
internal project that I

00:30:21.300 --> 00:30:24.240
worked on that is
currently in production

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:25.900
and can be used
internally by googlers.

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:27.750
And it was pretty awesome using
Stackdriver for the first time

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.940
because I wouldn't have
actually found those edge

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:31.350
cases in that scenario.

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:34.860
I'm actually kind of curious if
there are any really impressive

00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:38.400
use cases that you've heard
any of our customers using

00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:40.205
or any developers
that you've met.

00:30:40.205 --> 00:30:42.330
I'm not sure if you can
touch on that a little bit.

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:46.210
RORY PETTY: Again, some of these
are on the GCP YouTube channel.

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:49.050
But this year at Next, we
had quite a few sessions

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:51.570
that were kind of
co-led with customers.

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:54.210
So Samsung did a
session on how they

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:57.660
use Stackdriver to
monitor the performance

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:58.980
of a lot of their systems.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:02.090
Obviously, Samsung is a huge
electronics conglomerate,

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:05.352
so they have a huge variety
of web-based services

00:31:05.352 --> 00:31:06.810
that they have to
run and maintain.

00:31:06.810 --> 00:31:08.760
So they talked about
how they use Stackdriver

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:13.000
to calculate SLOs for their
services, crack error budgets,

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:14.050
things like that.

00:31:14.050 --> 00:31:16.950
So, yeah, there were quite
